# Conditional styling with `cn()`

Use `cn()` (clsx + tailwind-merge) whenever `className` combines a base string with conditionals, props, or variants. Do not use template literals, string concatenation, or `.filter(Boolean).join(" ")` for Tailwind classes.

## Imports

| Package | Import |
|---------|--------|
| `apps/web` | `import { cn } from "@/lib/utils"` |
| `packages/ui` | `import { cn } from "@workspace/ui/lib/utils"` |

## Do

```tsx
<div
  className={cn(
    "flex gap-md p-sm",
    isActive && "bg-accent",
    disabled && "pointer-events-none opacity-50",
    className
  )}
/>
```

- Pass `className` from props last so callers can override.
- Use object form for named flags: `cn("base", { "text-primary": selected })`.
- Prefer `cn()` even for static-only classes when the component already accepts `className`.

## Don't

```tsx
className={`flex ${isActive ? "bg-accent" : ""}`}
className={["typeset", preset, className].filter(Boolean).join(" ")}
className="p-sm " + (error ? "border-destructive" : "")
```

## Why

`twMerge` resolves conflicting Tailwind utilities (e.g. `p-sm` vs `p-md`) in one `className`. Template strings leave both classes on the element and produce unpredictable styles.
